[[:db/retract 17592186118334 :answer-field/choices 17592186118336] {:db/id 17592186119763 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-41bae79a-9c3e-5b74-b3cc-213f841b749e" "question-8a0a2c2f-1706-5f21-a00b-714f5830e8bd" "question-62dfa070-b095-529f-9742-123438c631da"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119761 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-37e058b7-fa62-5f7d-a443-fba67e4c6b5a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119762 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0e3e184e-e905-5d9a-a39c-5d0db9ad9647"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-41bae79a-9c3e-5b74-b3cc-213f841b749e" :question/id #uuid "41bae79a-9c3e-5b74-b3cc-213f841b749e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162415" :question/problem "The curvature of the plane curve $r(t)=t\\,i+\\frac{1}{t+1}\\,j$ is given by \n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{2(t+1)^{3}}{\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}},\\,t>-1.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\,y(t)=\\frac{1}{t+1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|1\\cdot \\frac{2}{(t+1)^{3}}-0\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}})|}{{({1}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}})}^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{3}}|}{{(1+\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{4}})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{3}}|}{\\sqrt{{[\\frac{(t+1)^{4}+1}{(t+1)^{4}}]}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(t+1)^{6}}{|(t+1)^{3}|\\sqrt{{((t+1)^{4}+1)}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(t+1)^{6}}{(t+1)^{3}\\sqrt{{((t+1)^{4}+1)}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(t+1)^{3}}{\\sqrt{{((t+1)^{4}+1)}^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we removed the absolute value because $t>-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-227607dc-c995-56fe-a20c-664bdc03dd83"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-227607dc-c995-56fe-a20c-664bdc03dd83" :answer-field/id #uuid "227607dc-c995-56fe-a20c-664bdc03dd83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-514f5bcf-ca33-5ef0-a733-ec86dffae285" :answer-field/choices ["answer-da852d6f-b131-56d4-8256-fdebdbc61a5a" "answer-8538bef5-2a64-541e-8605-5f22bd698faa" "answer-b649dd5a-85ff-5b4b-adbd-8d96f4510c5a" "answer-514f5bcf-ca33-5ef0-a733-ec86dffae285"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da852d6f-b131-56d4-8256-fdebdbc61a5a" :answer/id #uuid "da852d6f-b131-56d4-8256-fdebdbc61a5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{(t+1)^{2}+1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8538bef5-2a64-541e-8605-5f22bd698faa" :answer/id #uuid "8538bef5-2a64-541e-8605-5f22bd698faa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{(t+1)^{3}+1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b649dd5a-85ff-5b4b-adbd-8d96f4510c5a" :answer/id #uuid "b649dd5a-85ff-5b4b-adbd-8d96f4510c5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{{((t+1)^{2}+1)}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-514f5bcf-ca33-5ef0-a733-ec86dffae285" :answer/id #uuid "514f5bcf-ca33-5ef0-a733-ec86dffae285" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{{((t+1)^{4}+1)}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281461 :question/problem "The curvature of the plane curve $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+2t\\,j$ is given by \n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{2{e}^{t}}{\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAA}}}.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-8a0a2c2f-1706-5f21-a00b-714f5830e8bd" :question/id #uuid "8a0a2c2f-1706-5f21-a00b-714f5830e8bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-162419" :question/problem "The signed curvature of the plane curve $r(t)=⟨1-2t,\\,{e}^{2t}⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}}{\\sqrt{(1+{e}^{4t})^{3}}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=1-2t,\\,y(t)={e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =-2,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =4{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(t) & =\\frac{-2\\cdot 4{e}^{2t}-0\\cdot 2{e}^{2t}}{{((-2)^{2}+(2{e}^{2t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8{e}^{2t}}{{(4+4{e}^{4t})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8{e}^{2t}}{8{(1+{e}^{4t})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{e}^{2t}}{\\sqrt{(1+{e}^{4t})^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-13db80f1-1ccc-58f1-8641-3684ab9f70ba"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-13db80f1-1ccc-58f1-8641-3684ab9f70ba" :answer-field/id #uuid "13db80f1-1ccc-58f1-8641-3684ab9f70ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-16c29e5d-b1a7-5613-96fd-eb90123329c2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-18321e2d-03ab-5b7f-b6cf-eb257b7975d8" "answer-16c29e5d-b1a7-5613-96fd-eb90123329c2" "answer-7dd01b5f-502e-5b84-8ab6-aef88da96da0" "answer-76ff0fbe-7e33-5c49-a981-0cf35543aabc" "answer-8fab1247-6445-5427-bfd4-c38eeb5e52b8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18321e2d-03ab-5b7f-b6cf-eb257b7975d8" :answer/id #uuid "18321e2d-03ab-5b7f-b6cf-eb257b7975d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2{e}^{2t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16c29e5d-b1a7-5613-96fd-eb90123329c2" :answer/id #uuid "16c29e5d-b1a7-5613-96fd-eb90123329c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{e}^{2t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7dd01b5f-502e-5b84-8ab6-aef88da96da0" :answer/id #uuid "7dd01b5f-502e-5b84-8ab6-aef88da96da0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{e}^{2t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76ff0fbe-7e33-5c49-a981-0cf35543aabc" :answer/id #uuid "76ff0fbe-7e33-5c49-a981-0cf35543aabc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{2t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8fab1247-6445-5427-bfd4-c38eeb5e52b8" :answer/id #uuid "8fab1247-6445-5427-bfd4-c38eeb5e52b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{4t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-62dfa070-b095-529f-9742-123438c631da" :question/id #uuid "62dfa070-b095-529f-9742-123438c631da" :question/math-academy-id "q-162416" :question/problem "The signed curvature of the plane curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t}⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{2}{\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAA}}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "The signed curvature $k(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{t},\\,y(t)={e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =-{e}^{-t} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(t) & =\\frac{{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}-(-{e}^{-t})\\cdot {e}^{t}}{{(({e}^{t})^{2}+({e}^{-t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{{({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t})^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d781f999-1594-5949-8737-c9a323ba0c9c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d781f999-1594-5949-8737-c9a323ba0c9c" :answer-field/id #uuid "d781f999-1594-5949-8737-c9a323ba0c9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3478a58b-9f79-5e34-a211-77269420ac07" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3478a58b-9f79-5e34-a211-77269420ac07" "answer-b42b656a-892d-51db-80a8-a81810a225fa" "answer-4d339910-47cb-5c30-93e5-6488d858b18e" "answer-a4a98d71-c58f-5d5f-a189-b5539ed24ce8" "answer-037e0218-2ccb-5fc5-a7d9-90d6fd723b88"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3478a58b-9f79-5e34-a211-77269420ac07" :answer/id #uuid "3478a58b-9f79-5e34-a211-77269420ac07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t})^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b42b656a-892d-51db-80a8-a81810a225fa" :answer/id #uuid "b42b656a-892d-51db-80a8-a81810a225fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{({e}^{t}+{e}^{-t})^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d339910-47cb-5c30-93e5-6488d858b18e" :answer/id #uuid "4d339910-47cb-5c30-93e5-6488d858b18e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2{e}^{t}+2{e}^{-t}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4a98d71-c58f-5d5f-a189-b5539ed24ce8" :answer/id #uuid "a4a98d71-c58f-5d5f-a189-b5539ed24ce8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-037e0218-2ccb-5fc5-a7d9-90d6fd723b88" :answer/id #uuid "037e0218-2ccb-5fc5-a7d9-90d6fd723b88" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+{e}^{-t}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186401350 :question/problem "The signed curvature of the plane curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-{t}^{2}⟩$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t)=\\frac{2\\cdot (\\,\\bbox[2pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}}\\,)}{\\sqrt{({cos}^{2}⁡t+4{t}^{2})^{3}}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-37e058b7-fa62-5f7d-a443-fba67e4c6b5a" :question/id #uuid "37e058b7-fa62-5f7d-a443-fba67e4c6b5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162422" :question/problem "Find the radius of the curvature for the curve $r(t)=⟨(1-t)^{2},\\,{e}^{t}⟩$ at $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=(1-t)^{2},\\,y(t)={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =-2(1-t),\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & ={e}^{t} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =2,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|-2(1-t)\\cdot {e}^{t}-{e}^{t}\\cdot 2|}{({(-2(1-t))^{2}+({e}^{t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2t{e}^{t}-4{e}^{t}|}{({(2(1-t))^{2}+({e}^{t})^{2})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{|2(0){e}^{(0)}-4{e}^{(0)}|}{({(2(1-(0)))^{2}+({e}^{(0)})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{5}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0dbf3265-830d-57eb-a3a8-4ed3e1aae9cd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0dbf3265-830d-57eb-a3a8-4ed3e1aae9cd" :answer-field/id #uuid "0dbf3265-830d-57eb-a3a8-4ed3e1aae9cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0c476023-3edd-5454-ad4c-d620372551a6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c9d40450-bdc6-5d3a-b702-e8236d07b93e" "answer-0c476023-3edd-5454-ad4c-d620372551a6" "answer-5b451fb0-b40b-5c70-b25e-e6d23ad872c3" "answer-f0de606c-6689-5321-a410-2a40373d9b5e" "answer-d8b8ff58-1e96-5cc3-b54c-7bc562e920f3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9d40450-bdc6-5d3a-b702-e8236d07b93e" :answer/id #uuid "c9d40450-bdc6-5d3a-b702-e8236d07b93e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c476023-3edd-5454-ad4c-d620372551a6" :answer/id #uuid "0c476023-3edd-5454-ad4c-d620372551a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b451fb0-b40b-5c70-b25e-e6d23ad872c3" :answer/id #uuid "5b451fb0-b40b-5c70-b25e-e6d23ad872c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f0de606c-6689-5321-a410-2a40373d9b5e" :answer/id #uuid "f0de606c-6689-5321-a410-2a40373d9b5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8b8ff58-1e96-5cc3-b54c-7bc562e920f3" :answer/id #uuid "d8b8ff58-1e96-5cc3-b54c-7bc562e920f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120370 :question/problem "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)=(1-t)\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j$ at $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=1-t,\\,y(t)={e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =-1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & ={e}^{t} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & ={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|(-1)\\cdot {e}^{t}-({e}^{t})\\cdot 0|}{{((-1)^{2}+({e}^{t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{(1+{e}^{2t})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{{e}^{0}}{{(1+{e}^{2(0)})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(1+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186118334 :answer-field/correct 17592186118335 :answer-field/choices ["answer-bc367fc5-9c04-507f-a154-cf7c0d7dcc35"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc367fc5-9c04-507f-a154-cf7c0d7dcc35" :answer/id #uuid "bc367fc5-9c04-507f-a154-cf7c0d7dcc35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(1+e)^{3/2}}{e}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0e3e184e-e905-5d9a-a39c-5d0db9ad9647" :question/id #uuid "0e3e184e-e905-5d9a-a39c-5d0db9ad9647" :question/math-academy-id "q-162524" :question/problem "Find the curvature of the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ at $x=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(x)=\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\, & {y}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|}{{[1+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}^{2}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\pi ) & =\\frac{|-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi |}{{[1+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|1-0|}{{[1+(0+1)^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b69e114a-e3b9-5a0f-8073-62abdcb55456"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b69e114a-e3b9-5a0f-8073-62abdcb55456" :answer-field/id #uuid "b69e114a-e3b9-5a0f-8073-62abdcb55456" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e634d627-dd7c-5af7-b31a-020ab478d655" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8b8c1238-64a6-5834-b740-28291402e0cf" "answer-af28de7d-ae99-5116-bc25-465dc14a4553" "answer-6d9ba14f-0d41-5eb4-ad9c-a3ca3c24f04a" "answer-9bd14e87-92c1-5247-b76f-922de0ae6d11" "answer-e634d627-dd7c-5af7-b31a-020ab478d655"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b8c1238-64a6-5834-b740-28291402e0cf" :answer/id #uuid "8b8c1238-64a6-5834-b740-28291402e0cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af28de7d-ae99-5116-bc25-465dc14a4553" :answer/id #uuid "af28de7d-ae99-5116-bc25-465dc14a4553" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d9ba14f-0d41-5eb4-ad9c-a3ca3c24f04a" :answer/id #uuid "6d9ba14f-0d41-5eb4-ad9c-a3ca3c24f04a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9bd14e87-92c1-5247-b76f-922de0ae6d11" :answer/id #uuid "9bd14e87-92c1-5247-b76f-922de0ae6d11" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e634d627-dd7c-5af7-b31a-020ab478d655" :answer/id #uuid "e634d627-dd7c-5af7-b31a-020ab478d655" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121011 :tutorial/content "For a plane curve $C$ defined by the vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle x(t), \\: y(t) \\rangle,\n$$\n\nthe *curvature* of $C$ at an arbitrary point can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t)  = \\dfrac{|x'(t)y''(t) - y'(t)x''(t)|}{ (x'(t)^2 + y'(t)^2)^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, the **signed curvature** $k$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(t) = \\dfrac{x'(t)y''(t) - y'(t)x''(t)}{ (x'(t)^2 + y'(t)^2)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider a curve defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx(t) = 2t, \\qquad\ny(t) = 1+t^2.\n$$\n\nLet's find the curvature of this curve at the point where $t=1.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the first and second derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{′}(t)=2,\\, & {y}^{′}(t)=2t \\\\  & {x}^{″}(t)=0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these expressions into our curvature formula, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2\\cdot 2-2t\\cdot 0|}{{[{2}^{2}+(2t)^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,4\\,|}{{2}^{3}{[1+{t}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{({t}^{2}+1)}^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t =1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =\\frac{1}{2{({1}^{2}+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121013 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a plane curve defined by the explicit equation \n\n$$\ny = y(x).\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can parametrize this curve as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle t, \\: y(t) \\rangle.\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $x=t,$ we calculate the first and second derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =1,\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′}(x) \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =0,\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & ={y}^{″}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the (unsigned) curvature, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(x) & =\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|1\\cdot {y}^{″}(x)-{y}^{′}(x)\\cdot 0|}{({1}^{2}+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|{y}^{″}(x)|}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unsigned curvature for $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa (x) = \\dfrac{|y''(x)|}{ (1 + [y'(x)]^2)^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nand the signed curvature is\n\n$$\nk(x) = \\dfrac {y''(x)} {(1+[y'(x)]^2)^{3/2}}\t.\n$$\n\nNote that the signed curvature $k(x)$ has the same sign as $y''(x).$ Therefore,\n\n- if $k(x) > 0,$ the curve is concave up, and\n\n- if $k(x) < 0,$ the curve is concave down." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121012 :tutorial/content "We've been using the following formula for unsigned curvature:\n\n$$\n\\kappa (t)  = \\dfrac{|x'(t)y''(t) - y'(t)x''(t)|}{ (x'(t)^2 + y'(t)^2)^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nTo see where this formula comes from, remember that for a curve $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle x(t),  y(t),  z(t)\\rangle,$ the unsigned curvature formula is\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\|\\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t)\\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}.\n$$\n\nIf a curve does not have a $z$-component, then we may consider $z(t) = 0.$ Thus, any plane curve $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle x(t), \\: y(t) \\rangle$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle x(t), \\:  y(t), \\: 0\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\mathbf r''(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t) = \\langle x'(t), y'(t),0 \\rangle, \\qquad \\mathbf r''(t) = \\langle x''(t), y''(y),0 \\rangle\n$$\n\nSubstituting these expressions into our cross-product formula, we find that the curvature $\\kappa(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{‖⟨{x}^{′}(t),{y}^{′}(t),0⟩\\times ⟨{x}^{″}(t),{y}^{″}(t),0⟩‖}{‖⟨{x}^{′}(t),{y}^{′}(t)⟩{‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{‖⟨0,0,{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)⟩‖}{(\\sqrt{{x}^{′}(t)^{2}+{y}^{′}(t)^{2}})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{({x}^{′}(t)^{2}+{y}^{′}(t)^{2})^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186120038 :activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
